// ============================================================================
// EXAMPLE CHAIN: Complex feature implementation, TDD-first
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Precondition: this chain executes a plan a human has already read and APPROVED
// (Claude-Code-style plan gate). The chain therefore does NOT re-litigate scope
// or ask for approval mid-run. It assumes the approved plan is passed in as {task}.
//
// Feature under implementation (illustrative):
//   "Add idempotency-key support to POST /payments so retried requests with the
//    same Idempotency-Key header return the original response instead of double-
//    charging. Keys expire after 24h. Concurrent duplicates must not race."
//
// Design principles encoded below:
//   1. Understand before touching code (scout + structured handoff).
//   2. TDD: write failing tests FIRST, implement to green, then refactor.
//   3. Never let one agent both write the tests and grade its own passing.
//   4. Parallel review across independent axes (correctness / tests / security).
//   5. Structured outputs at every handoff boundary so downstream agents get
//      machine-checkable data, not prose they have to re-parse.
//   6. Worktree isolation only where parallel agents would edit the same tree.
//   7. Document what SHIPPED (incl. deferrals), as the final phase, not the plan.
// ============================================================================

{
  chain: [

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // PHASE 1 — CONTEXT
    // Map the code before changing it. Read-only. Emits a strict schema so the
    // planner and TDD steps receive concrete file paths and seams, not a vibe.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      agent: "scout",
      phase: "Context",
      label: "Map payments write path",
      as: "context",
      task: `Approved plan:
{task}

Do READ-ONLY recon of the payment-creation path relevant to adding idempotency.
Do not edit code. Identify:
- the request entry point(s) for POST /payments and their handler files
- where the DB write / charge side-effect happens (the thing we must not double-run)
- existing middleware, error types, and response-shape conventions to match
- current test files + framework + how tests are run
- any existing idempotency / dedup / caching primitives already in the repo
- concurrency risks (transactions, locks, connection pooling) at the write site
- where docs live (README, /docs, API reference) and their format`,
      outputSchema: {
        type: "object",
        required: ["entry_points", "side_effect_site", "test_setup", "conventions", "risks"],
        properties: {
          entry_points:   { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } }, // file:line
          side_effect_site: {
            type: "object",
            required: ["file", "description"],
            properties: {
              file:        { type: "string" },
              description: { type: "string" }
            }
          },
          test_setup: {
            type: "object",
            required: ["framework", "run_command", "test_dirs"],
            properties: {
              framework:   { type: "string" },
              run_command: { type: "string" },      // e.g. "npm test -- payments"
              test_dirs:   { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } }
            }
          },
          existing_primitives: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } },
          conventions:  { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } },
          risks:        { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } },
          docs_locations: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } }
        }
      }
    },

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // PHASE 2 — PLAN → TEST MATRIX
    // Turn the approved plan + context into a concrete, ordered test matrix.
    // This is the contract the whole TDD loop implements against. Read-only.
    // We derive the tests HERE, separately, so the implementer doesn't get to
    // define its own definition of "done".
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      agent: "planner",
      phase: "Plan",
      label: "Derive test matrix",
      as: "testplan",
      task: `Approved plan:
{task}

Repo context (structured):
{outputs.context}

Produce the TEST MATRIX we will implement TDD-style. For each behavior give a
test id, a plain-English assertion, the layer (unit|integration), and whether it
is a happy path, edge case, or failure/concurrency case. Cover at minimum:
- first request with a key: charges normally, stores response under the key
- retry with same key + same body: returns the STORED response, does NOT re-charge
- retry with same key + DIFFERENT body: rejected as a conflict (do not silently
  return the old response)
- missing/absent key: falls back to current non-idempotent behavior
- key TTL: an expired key behaves like a fresh request
- concurrency: two simultaneous requests with the same key must not both charge
Do NOT write code. Output only the matrix + a short note on the smallest slice
to implement first.`,
      outputSchema: {
        type: "object",
        required: ["tests", "first_slice"],
        properties: {
          tests: {
            type: "array",
            items: {
              type: "object",
              required: ["id", "assertion", "layer", "kind"],
              properties: {
                id:        { type: "string" },
                assertion: { type: "string" },
                layer:     { type: "string", enum: ["unit", "integration"] },
                kind:      { type: "string", enum: ["happy", "edge", "failure", "concurrency"] }
              }
            }
          },
          first_slice: { type: "string" }
        }
      }
    },

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // PHASE 3 — RED
    // Write the failing tests from the matrix. Nothing else. The tests MUST run
    // and MUST fail for the right reason (feature missing), not because of typos
    // or import errors. Forked context so it inherits the working tree state.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      agent: "worker",
      phase: "TDD:Red",
      label: "Write failing tests",
      as: "red",
      context: "fork",
      task: `Test matrix:
{outputs.testplan}

Repo context:
{outputs.context}

Write the tests described in the matrix, matching the repo's existing framework
and conventions ({outputs.context}). RULES:
- Write ONLY test code. Do not implement the feature.
- Every new test must fail because the behavior does not exist yet — not because
  of a syntax/import/setup error. Run the suite and confirm each new test fails
  for the intended reason.
- Return the exact command you ran, the list of new test files/cases, and the
  observed failure output (trimmed) proving they are red.`,
      outputSchema: {
        type: "object",
        required: ["run_command", "new_tests", "all_red"],
        properties: {
          run_command: { type: "string" },
          new_tests:   { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } },
          all_red:     { type: "boolean" },
          failure_excerpt: { type: "string" }
        }
      }
    },

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // PHASE 4 — GREEN
    // Implement the minimum production code to make the red tests pass. Do not
    // change the tests (except obviously-wrong ones, which must be flagged, not
    // silently rewritten to pass). Forked so it sees the tests written in Red.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      agent: "worker",
      phase: "TDD:Green",
      label: "Implement to green",
      as: "green",
      context: "fork",
      task: `Failing tests are in place:
{outputs.red}

Approved plan:
{task}

Context:
{outputs.context}

Implement the SMALLEST production change that makes all the new tests pass, plus
keeps the existing suite green. RULES:
- Do not weaken or delete tests to get to green. If a test is genuinely wrong,
  STOP and report it in "test_issues" instead of editing it to pass.
- Handle the concurrency case for real (transaction / unique constraint / lock),
  not with a sleep or a comment.
- Run '{outputs.red.run_command}' plus the full suite. Return both results.`,
      outputSchema: {
        type: "object",
        required: ["files_changed", "target_suite_pass", "full_suite_pass"],
        properties: {
          files_changed:     { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } },
          target_suite_pass: { type: "boolean" },
          full_suite_pass:   { type: "boolean" },
          test_issues:       { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } },
          notes:             { type: "string" }
        }
      }
    },

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // PHASE 5 — REFACTOR
    // Tests are green; now improve the implementation without changing behavior.
    // Suite must stay green the entire time. Still forked.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      agent: "worker",
      phase: "TDD:Refactor",
      label: "Refactor under green",
      as: "refactor",
      context: "fork",
      task: `Green implementation:
{outputs.green}

Refactor for clarity and to match repo conventions ({outputs.context.conventions})
WITHOUT changing observable behavior. Extract the idempotency logic into a
cohesive, testable unit if it's currently inline in the handler. Re-run the full
suite after every meaningful change; it must remain green. Return the final diff
summary and confirmation the suite is still green.`,
      outputSchema: {
        type: "object",
        required: ["full_suite_pass", "summary"],
        properties: {
          full_suite_pass: { type: "boolean" },
          files_changed:   { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } },
          summary:         { type: "string" }
        }
      }
    },

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // PHASE 6 — PARALLEL REVIEW (fan-out)
    // Three FRESH reviewers, independent axes, so no single reviewer's blind spot
    // decides the outcome. Fresh context (default) keeps them unbiased by the
    // implementer's rationale. Each returns structured findings we can merge.
    // failFast:false so one reviewer erroring doesn't kill the others.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      phase: "Review",
      parallel: [
        {
          agent: "reviewer",
          label: "Correctness & concurrency",
          as: "review_correctness",
          task: `Review the change for correctness against the approved plan:
{task}
and the test matrix: {outputs.testplan}.
Focus on the double-charge / race guarantee, TTL handling, and the same-key-
different-body conflict path. Files changed: {outputs.refactor.files_changed}.
Return structured findings.`,
          outputSchema: {
            type: "object",
            required: ["findings"],
            properties: {
              findings: {
                type: "array",
                items: {
                  type: "object",
                  required: ["severity", "location", "issue"],
                  properties: {
                    severity: { type: "string", enum: ["blocker", "major", "minor", "nit"] },
                    location: { type: "string" },
                    issue:    { type: "string" },
                    suggested_fix: { type: "string" }
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          agent: "reviewer",
          label: "Test quality",
          as: "review_tests",
          task: `Review TEST QUALITY only. Do the new tests actually pin the
behavior in {outputs.testplan}, or are any tautological / mocked into
meaninglessness? Is the concurrency test real? Any matrix rows with no
corresponding test? Return structured findings using the same schema.`,
          outputSchema: {
            type: "object",
            required: ["findings"],
            properties: {
              findings: {
                type: "array",
                items: {
                  type: "object",
                  required: ["severity", "location", "issue"],
                  properties: {
                    severity: { type: "string", enum: ["blocker", "major", "minor", "nit"] },
                    location: { type: "string" },
                    issue:    { type: "string" },
                    suggested_fix: { type: "string" }
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          agent: "reviewer",
          label: "Security & abuse",
          as: "review_security",
          task: `Security review: key collisions across tenants/users, unbounded
key storage, replay of stored responses leaking data across auth contexts,
injection via the Idempotency-Key header. Return structured findings using the
same schema.`,
          outputSchema: {
            type: "object",
            required: ["findings"],
            properties: {
              findings: {
                type: "array",
                items: {
                  type: "object",
                  required: ["severity", "location", "issue"],
                  properties: {
                    severity: { type: "string", enum: ["blocker", "major", "minor", "nit"] },
                    location: { type: "string" },
                    issue:    { type: "string" },
                    suggested_fix: { type: "string" }
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      concurrency: 3,
      failFast: false
    },

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // PHASE 7 — SYNTHESIZE & APPLY (fan-in)
    // Merge the three reviews, apply blockers+majors, re-run the suite, and stop
    // for anything that would exceed the approved plan's scope rather than
    // inventing new scope on its own.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      agent: "worker",
      phase: "Finalize",
      label: "Apply review feedback",
      as: "final",
      context: "fork",
      task: `Reviews:
- correctness: {outputs.review_correctness}
- tests:       {outputs.review_tests}
- security:    {outputs.review_security}

Apply every 'blocker' and 'major' finding. Apply 'minor'/'nit' only if cheap and
safe. RULES:
- If a finding would require work beyond the approved plan ({task}), do NOT
  silently expand scope: implement what's in-plan, and list the out-of-scope
  items under "deferred" for the human.
- Keep TDD discipline: if a fix needs new behavior, add a failing test first,
  then make it pass.
- Re-run the full suite. Return the final state.`,
      outputSchema: {
        type: "object",
        required: ["applied", "full_suite_pass", "deferred"],
        properties: {
          applied:         { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } },
          deferred:        { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } },
          files_changed:   { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } },
          full_suite_pass: { type: "boolean" },
          summary:         { type: "string" }
        }
      }
    },

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // PHASE 8 — DOCUMENT (last, deliberately)
    // Runs AFTER finalize so it documents what actually shipped — including
    // anything the finalize step deferred — not what the plan aspired to.
    // Fresh context: the writer should read the code/plan as the source of
    // truth rather than inherit the implementer's in-head justifications.
    // Writes concise docs for BOTH audiences: humans (API reference / changelog)
    // and agents (a machine-readable behavior contract future runs can rely on).
    // Constrained to doc surfaces + the docs_locations scout found, so it can't
    // quietly change production code. Structured output so the harness can
    // verify docs were actually written and where.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      agent: "technical-writer",
      phase: "Docs",
      label: "Document shipped behavior",
      as: "docs",
      task: `Approved plan:
{task}

What actually shipped:
- implementation summary: {outputs.refactor.summary}
- final changes + deferrals: {outputs.final}
- behavior contract (test matrix): {outputs.testplan}
- files touched: {outputs.final.files_changed}
- doc locations in repo: {outputs.context.docs_locations}

Create or UPDATE concise documentation for two audiences. Keep it tight — no
filler, match existing doc style.

1. HUMAN-FACING (in the repo's existing docs surfaces):
   - API reference for the Idempotency-Key header on POST /payments: semantics,
     the same-key/different-body 409 conflict, the 24h TTL, and the no-double-
     charge concurrency guarantee.
   - A short changelog / release-note entry.
2. AGENT-FACING (a concise machine-readable behavior contract, e.g. a docs/
   markdown block or structured note): the invariants a future agent must
   preserve — "same key + same body => stored response, no re-charge",
   "same key + different body => 409", "expired key => fresh request",
   "concurrent duplicates => at most one charge".

RULES:
- Edit ONLY documentation surfaces ({outputs.context.docs_locations}) and doc
  files you create. Do NOT modify production or test code.
- Document reality, including anything under {outputs.final.deferred}: note known
  limitations honestly rather than describing intended-but-unshipped behavior.
- Return the list of doc files created/updated and a one-line summary of each.`,
      outputSchema: {
        type: "object",
        required: ["docs_written", "human_docs", "agent_contract"],
        properties: {
          docs_written: {
            type: "array",
            items: {
              type: "object",
              required: ["path", "action", "summary"],
              properties: {
                path:    { type: "string" },
                action:  { type: "string", enum: ["created", "updated"] },
                summary: { type: "string" }
              }
            }
          },
          human_docs:     { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } }, // paths
          agent_contract: { type: "string" },                           // path
          limitations_documented: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } }
        }
      }
    }
  ],

  // No clarify UI, run start-to-finish: the human already approved the plan.
  clarify: false
}